WEBVTT

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:03.720
What's up for September?

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:04.750
Jupiter.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:06.350
Hello and welcome.

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:11.310
I'm Jane Houston Jones at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena, California.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.640
2009 is International Year of Astronomy.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.600
Each month this year we'll be showcasing a great celestial object, and this month it's

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.360
the planet Jupiter.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.560
We'll also be telling you about Juno, a mission to that giant planet, which launches in 2011.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.960
Jupiter is the brightest object in the East.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.760
evening sky this month. Through a telescope, you can see cloud bands on Jupiter. In July,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.900
a small comet or icy body crashed into Jupiter's southern polar area and left a black bruise.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:48.250
This new dark feature was discovered by Anthony Wesley, an amateur astronomer in Australia.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:54.720
This spot captivated Jupiter observers for over a month, while Jupiter's atmosphere distorted

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.980
its shape and it finally dissipated. This amazing feature has been imaged and

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.620
and studied by amateur and professional astronomers around the world.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:09.990
Jupiter also has four large satellites, three of which are larger than our own moon.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.240
These four moons were discovered by Galileo 400 years ago.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:20.430
You can see them yourself with a small telescope or even binoculars and watch them move around

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.600
the planet just as Galileo did.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.650
NASA's spacecraft named for Galileo ended its exploration of Jupiter six years ago.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.800
The next mission to Jupiter, called Juno, will launch in 2011.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.710
Juno will be the first solar-powered spacecraft to visit an outer planet

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.940
and the first to have a polar orbit around an outer planet.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.820
This gives Juno a unique view of the planet, including the polar auroras,

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.750
the northern and southern lights.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:59.910
It also lets the spacecraft get very close to Jupiter while avoiding the planet's

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.580
dangerous radiation belts. Juno will look for important clues about Jupiter's

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.480
formation by measuring how much water is there. It will also investigate the

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.430
planet's internal structure, searching for a central core, and will learn how

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:22.230
and where, inside the planet, Jupiter's powerful magnetic field is generated. The

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.270
mission will look deeper into Jupiter than we've ever been able to before, to

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.610
see how the planet's visible atmosphere and features

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.220
like the famous Great Red Spot, are shaped by currents in Jupiter's deep interior.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:41.070
Jupiter rules the evening skies this month, so go out and take a look.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.390
And Juno arrives at this king of the planets in 2016.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.540
There's one other object in the evening sky this month worth mentioning.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.330
The asteroid Juno, one of the first four asteroids ever discovered,

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.510
is bright enough to see with a pair of binoculars this month.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:02:59.650
All you have to know is where to look.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:05.760
You can learn all about NASA's missions at www.nasa.gov.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:09.310
That's all for this month. I'm Jane Houston Jones.
